#note[
*Scope.* Daytona Linux sandboxes (Firecracker microVMs) on four bare-metal hosts running the *same Daytona build*,
the same benchmark harness and the same recipes. Three measurements: *startup* (1,000 or 2,000 sandboxes created at once),
*throughput* (jobs per second of a CPU-bound agent workload running inside packed sandboxes) and *density* (sandboxes
alive at once). Plus a per-step breakdown of the create path, the fixes that changed the numbers, and the methodology
corrections behind them. All four hosts are development benchmark cells, not production.
]

= Executive summary

*Vera's real strength is fast cores under sustained CPU-bound sandbox work — but on our current data that lead is moderate,
not decisive, and it does not extend to startup, density or reliability.*

#table(columns: (1.2fr, 0.8fr, 3fr),
  [Question], [Leader], [Evidence],
  [Throughput (jobs/s)], [#w[Vera]#super[\*]], [Vera 26.7 · Graviton5 22.9 (+16 %) · EPYC 9575F 8.0 (×3.3, mostly core count: 176 vs 64 cores). Holds at 2,000 sandboxes (26.1 vs 8.0). #super[\*]Turin not yet measured.],
  [Speed per core], [#w[Vera]], [0.152 jobs/s per physical core vs 0.126 (9575F) and 0.119 (Graviton5): +21–28 %. Fastest single job (5.9 s vs 6.6–7.1 s). Per hardware thread Graviton5 leads (0.119 vs 0.076).],
  [Throughput per socket], [Graviton5], [Graviton5 22.9 on one socket vs Vera ≈ 13 per socket.],
  [Platform startup (idle image)], [9575F, Graviton5], [1k burst, typical sandbox, fixes on: Graviton5 0.85 s · 9575F 1.06 s · Vera 1.57 s · Turin 2.35 s.],
  [Startup under CPU load (agent image)], [Turin], [1k all ready: Turin 7.9 s · Graviton5 10–12 s · 9575F 19 s · Vera 22–28 s. 2k: Turin 21 s · Vera 50–55 s.],
  [Density], [Turin], [8,000 live cleanly, peak 9,750 (limited by the runner heartbeat, not hardware). Vera and the 9575F verified to 2,000.],
  [Reliability], [x86, Graviton5], [0 failures in thousands of startups. Vera: ≈ 0.2 % of guests wedge after snapshot resume (#3213).],
)

*Bottom line.* On Daytona's current sandbox benchmarks, Vera is *competitive* — fastest cores, steady clock under load,
highest measured throughput — but *not significantly ahead*, and slower at startup. Three tests could still change that
(section 9): Turin's throughput ladder, jobs per watt, and a memory-bandwidth-heavy sandbox workload, which is where
Vera's design should matter and which our CPU-bound benchmark does not exercise.

